This is the second primary feature.

Do not build a generic India-wide scheme finder.

Build a Karnataka-focused scheme discovery and eligibility system.

## A. INITIAL SCHEME COVERAGE

Start with a manageable, verified collection of schemes.

Priority categories:

1. Karnataka student scholarships.
2. Karnataka farmer welfare schemes.
3. Karnataka social welfare schemes.
4. Karnataka backward classes welfare schemes.
5. Karnataka women and child welfare schemes.
6. Selected central schemes available to Karnataka residents, such as PM-KISAN.

Initial source portals:

Karnataka SSP:
https://ssp.karnataka.gov.in/

Karnataka Social Welfare Department:
https://swd.karnataka.gov.in/

Karnataka Backward Classes Welfare Department:
https://bcwd.karnataka.gov.in/

Karnataka Agriculture:
https://raitamitra.karnataka.gov.in/

FRUITS:
https://fruits.karnataka.gov.in/

Karnataka Women and Child Development:
https://dwcd.karnataka.gov.in/

PM-KISAN:
https://pmkisan.gov.in/

PM-KISAN must be clearly labelled as a central government scheme available to eligible Karnataka farmers, not as a Karnataka state scheme.

Do not add large numbers of schemes using unverified information.

Begin with a smaller number of fully documented schemes.

If scheme-specific official rules cannot be obtained, do not activate that scheme in the eligibility engine.

## B. USER INFORMATION FORM

Collect only information required for relevant schemes.

Possible fields:

- Age.
- State (Karnataka).
- District.
- Annual family income.
- Occupation.
- Student / employed / unemployed / farmer.
- Education level.
- Institution type, where relevant.
- Category, only where required.
- Gender, only where required.
- Disability status, only where required.
- Farmer registration information, where relevant.

Make fields conditional.

For example, do not ask a student for land ownership information unless a particular scheme requires it.

Do not collect Aadhaar numbers, bank passwords, OTPs or other unnecessary sensitive identifiers.

## C. SCHEME DATABASE

Create a MongoDB collection called schemes.

Every scheme must have:

- scheme_id
- scheme_name
- scheme_category
- implementing_department
- state_or_central
- description
- benefits
- eligibility_rules
- exclusion_criteria
- required_documents
- application_process
- application_url
- official_guidelines_url
- source_ids
